WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
The first film showed growth slowing near carrying capacity.

2
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.060
But when a crab count falls, did the population move, did carrying capacity move, or

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.880
both? Return to the same creek.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.680
This time, follow a pair of trackers: teal for population size and gold for carrying

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
capacity.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.780
Population size means how many individuals are present now.

7
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.200
Carrying capacity means the largest population the environment can support over

8
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.120
time. The trackers can move together, but they are not the same measurement.

9
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.440
A low population can sit below either a high or low capacity.

10
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.560
Begin with a brief cold event in this schematic creek.

11
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.060
Some teal crabs disappear, so population size falls.

12
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.300
Food, space, and shelter remain.

13
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.780
Under that assumption, the gold support level stays put.

14
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.300
The population may grow back toward it after the event passes.

15
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:08.583
Now change the creek for the long term.

16
00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:12.523
Remove much of the green shelter and keep it gone.

17
00:01:13.963 --> 00:01:18.623
Young crabs have fewer hiding places, so in this model the environment supports

18
00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:20.103
fewer crabs over time.

19
00:01:21.363 --> 00:01:26.863
The gold carrying-capacity tracker moves down, and population growth now slows near

20
00:01:26.863 --> 00:01:27.863
that lower level.

21
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:30.127
Here is the prediction.

22
00:01:31.607 --> 00:01:34.047
Both windows now show the same low crab count.

23
00:01:35.307 --> 00:01:36.867
The left followed a brief event.

24
00:01:38.027 --> 00:01:40.087
The right lost shelter for the long term.

25
00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:44.507
Can the count alone tell you whether carrying capacity moved?

26
00:01:45.887 --> 00:01:46.887
Hold your answer.

27
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:57.030
No. The same abundance can come from different paths.

28
00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.250
To tell them apart, scientists need evidence about food, habitat, predators,

29
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.410
weather, and survival across time.

30
00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.410
The count shows population size.

31
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.870
It does not measure carrying capacity or identify which limiting factor caused a

32
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:15.870
change.

33
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:18.503
That matters for the winter dredge survey.

34
00:02:20.003 --> 00:02:23.923
Crews sample buried crabs across the Bay and build a Bay-wide estimate.

35
00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:27.243
The estimate is not an exact count.

36
00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:31.163
It can show whether reported groups rose or fell.

37
00:02:32.403 --> 00:02:34.743
By itself, it cannot show why they changed.

38
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:40.427
A lasting gain in food or shelter can raise environmental support.

39
00:02:41.887 --> 00:02:43.907
Lasting habitat loss can lower it.

40
00:02:44.927 --> 00:02:46.827
People can affect both trackers too.

41
00:02:48.247 --> 00:02:52.147
Harvest removes individuals, while shoreline building can remove marsh.

42
00:02:53.527 --> 00:02:58.407
Removing individuals does not automatically prove that the support level changed.

43
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:01.487
Return to the paired trackers.

44
00:03:02.547 --> 00:03:06.247
A brief shock moved the teal population while gold support stayed.

45
00:03:07.647 --> 00:03:10.267
Lasting shelter loss moved the gold level too.

46
00:03:11.587 --> 00:03:14.567
Keep the distinction whenever a graph rises or falls.

47
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:19.727
Ask what changed, how long it lasted, and what evidence measured it.
